        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did Andrew Worster work for?\n\nAnswer A: McMaster University Faculty of Health Sciences\n  about: Andrew Worster\n  sense: researcher\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Andrew Worster has worked as a researcher at the McMaster University Faculty of Health Sciences, a renowned medical school in Canada.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Northeastern University\n  about: Andrew Worster\n  sense: researcher\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Andrew Worster is a renowned researcher who has made significant contributions to various fields of study. One of the most notable aspects of his career is his affiliation with Northeastern University, a private university located in Boston, Massachusetts, USA.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Andrew Worster\n\nAndrew Worster is a researcher who has made significant contributions to the field of medicine. One of his notable affiliations is with the McMaster University Faculty of Health Sciences, a prestigious medical school in Canada. Career\n\nAndrew Worster has had a distinguished career as a researcher, and his work has been recognized and respected by the academic community. One of the most notable aspects of his career is his association with the McMaster University Faculty of Health Sciences. Association with McMaster University Faculty of Health Sciences\n\nAndrew Worster has worked as a researcher at the McMaster University Faculty of Health Sciences, a renowned medical school in Canada. This faculty is known for its excellence in medical education, research, and patient care. Worster's work at the faculty has contributed significantly to the advancement of medical knowledge and practice. Evidence of Association\n\nMultiple credible sources confirm Andrew Worster's association with the McMaster University Faculty of Health Sciences. According to his profile on the McMaster University website, Worster is listed as a faculty member at the Department of Medicine, Faculty of Health Sciences. [1] Additionally, several research papers and publications co-authored by Worster have been affiliated with the McMaster University Faculty of Health Sciences, further substantiating his connection to the institution. [2][3]\n\nReferences:\n\n[1] McMaster University. (n.d.). Faculty Profile: Andrew Worster. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/fhs.mcmaster.ca\\/medicine\\/faculty_member\\/worster_andrew\\/>\n\n[2] Worster, A., & et al. (2018). Diagnostic accuracy of emergency department clinical decisions regarding admission or discharge of patients with acute chest pain. CJEM, 20(3), 341-348. doi: 10.1017\\/cem.2017.436\n\n[3] Worster, A., & et al. (2020). The McMaster Acute Coronary Syndromes (MACS) Rule: A prospective cohort study.\nDocument 2: Andrew Worster\n\nAndrew Worster is a renowned researcher who has made significant contributions to various fields of study. One of the most notable aspects of his career is his affiliation with Northeastern University, a private university located in Boston, Massachusetts, USA. Early Career and Education\n\nAndrew Worster received his undergraduate degree in Biological Sciences from Harvard University in 1995. He then went on to pursue his graduate studies at the University of California, Berkeley, where he earned his Ph.D. in Microbiology in 2001. Academic Career\n\nWorster's academic career began in 2002 when he joined the faculty at Northeastern University as a Research Assistant Professor in the Department of Biology. During his tenure at the university, he conducted extensive research on the molecular mechanisms of bacterial pathogenesis, publishing numerous papers in peer-reviewed journals such as the Journal of Bacteriology and Infection and Immunity. Notable Studies\n\nOne of Worster's most notable studies during his time at Northeastern University was his investigation into the role of quorum sensing in Pseudomonas aeruginosa biofilm formation. This study, published in the journal Science in 2005, demonstrated the importance of quorum sensing in the development of antibiotic-resistant biofilms and has since been cited over 500 times. Another significant study conducted by Worster during his time at Northeastern University was his examination of the effects of environmental pollutants on microbial communities. This study, published in the journal Environmental Science and Technology in 2008, highlighted the impact of industrial pollutants on the diversity and function of microbial ecosystems. Awards and Honors\n\nWorster's contributions to the field of microbiology have been recognized through several awards and honors. In 2007, he was awarded the National Science Foundation's CAREER Award for his research on the molecular mechanisms of bacterial pathogenesis. He has also been named a Fellow of the American Academy of Microbiology. References\n\n* Worster, A. (2005). Quorum sensing and biofilm formation in Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Science, 308(5724), 1183-1186. * Worster, A., & Smith, J. (2008). Effects of environmental pollutants on microbial communities. Environmental Science and Technology, 42(19), 7315-7321. * National Science Foundation. (2007). CAREER Award Recipients.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
